  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 246 PM MDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nCurrent observations show temperatures in the 50s and 60s, and \nmostly sunny skies east of the Continental Divide. A few showers \nhave developed over western Colorado and parts of Park County, \nalthough these should be short-lived. Decreasing cloud cover over\nthe mountains and clear skies for the plains late tonight will \ngive way to one more chilly morning (though still above average) \nwith morning lows in the upper 30s to low 40s across the low \nelevations, and 30s for the mountains.\n\nTomorrow, a shortwave will continue to drift through the area and \nallow for a few light showers over the high terrain once again in \nthe afternoon. For the plains and Denver metro, 70-degree afternoon \nhighs make a return with a few patches of mid level clouds and \nperhaps some virga. Surface winds are expected to generally remain \nlight at all levels through at least tomorrow evening due to the \nweak pressure gradient and light southwest flow aloft. \n\nThe synoptic pattern becomes more amplified on Wednesday as an \nupper level ridge builds over just east of our area and a strong \ncutoff low approaches the California coast. A subtropical jet \nahead of this cutoff low will strengthen southwesterly flow aloft \nand begin advecting Pacific moisture towards the Four Corners \nWednesday evening into Thursday. This increase in moisture will \nallow rain showers to become more numerous over the mountains \nWednesday evening, although the heaviest rain should remain well \noutside of our CWA, over southwest Colorado. In terms of rainfall \namounts, a trace up to 0.25\" of QPF seems reasonable for the \nmountains, with the Denver metro and plains remaining dry. The \nairmass appears to be too warm for any tangible snow \naccumulations, with perhaps only some dustings above 12,000 ft. It\nis worth noting that moisture transport with this system is quite\nimpressive. Long-range ensemble means show precipitable water \n(PW) values reaching the 0.60\" to 0.90\" range, which is well above\nthen 90th percentile and even the daily maximum of climatology \nfor this time of year. However, with such limited instability \naround (in comparison to peak monsoon season), we expect the bulk \nof the rainfall to be orographic in nature, coming to fruition \nover areas where southwesterly flow is a favorable upslope \norientation (i.e. primarily southwest Colorado). With so much high\nterrain blocking the bulk of this plume of Pacific moisture, high\nclouds may be all that the foothills, Denver metro, and plains \nget out of this system. \n\nRegarding temperatures, there is pretty good agreement in \ntemperatures staying warmer than seasonable through at least \nSaturday, with the Denver metro and plains potentially reaching \nthe mid to upper 70s Wednesday through Friday. A cold front is \nstill expected to push through the area on Saturday, cooling \ntemperatures down to near-average for the weekend.",
